1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,971
Two business owners, same one hundred eighty thousand dollars in profit.

2
00:00:05,011 --> 00:00:10,785
One pays about fifteen thousand dollars less tax every year, and it's completely legal.

3
00:00:10,825 --> 00:00:11,560
Here's why.

4
00:00:12,467 --> 00:00:19,035
A default LLC pays self-employment tax, fifteen point three percent, on almost all of that profit.

5
00:00:19,075 --> 00:00:21,727
That's about twenty-five thousand dollars.

6
00:00:22,833 --> 00:00:27,044
An S corp owner pays that same rate only on a reasonable salary.

7
00:00:27,084 --> 00:00:30,962
At sixty-eight thousand, that's about ten thousand dollars.

8
00:00:31,002 --> 00:00:33,393
The rest comes out as distributions.

9
00:00:34,566 --> 00:00:41,600
The catch: your salary has to be reasonable for the work, and an S corp costs a few thousand a year to run.

10
00:00:41,640 --> 00:00:45,368
In this example, that still leaves about twelve thousand.

11
00:00:45,408 --> 00:00:47,126
But it's not for everyone.

12
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:55,036
The full breakdown, with the math and who it actually fits, is on the channel.

13
00:00:55,076 --> 00:00:59,180
Educational only, not tax advice, so check with a tax professional.
